UIKit: input hooks, relative mouse mode and render scale for Voxelium.

Voxelium draws its own touch controls over SDL's window and turns touches
into keyboard and mouse input. SDL_Voxelium* (SDL_uikitvoxelium.m) send
that input through SDL's keyboard and mouse code, so SDL_GetKeyboardState,
SDL_GetModState and the button state stay in step with the events, as for
real devices. Minecraft 26.3 reads key state with SDL_GetKeyboardState.
SDL_VoxeliumRequestClose sends the window a close request (the controls'
"Quit game"), which Minecraft handles like its window's close button.

Without a GCMouse (and UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents) UIKit had
no SetRelativeMouseMode, so Minecraft's SDL_SetWindowRelativeMouseMode
failed ("That operation is not supported"). Relative mode now always
succeeds; SDL_VoxeliumRelativeMouseMode tells the app whether the game has
captured the mouse (in the world) or not (menus).

High-pixel-density windows (Minecraft's) use the screen's native scale
times VOXELIUM_RENDER_SCALE (0.1 - 1, default 1) for the Metal view and
SDL_GetWindowSizeInPixels, so the Vulkan swapchain follows the launcher's
render-resolution setting.
The Metal view's scale is set through contentScaleFactor: UIKit resets a
layer's contentsScale to the screen scale when its view joins a window, so
the swapchain stayed full size while SDL reported the scaled size, and taps
landed at half their position (iPhone, build 202610232310).

A new window takes the screen's size without an event, and the later
resize from the view controller is dropped when the size did not change
(game started in landscape). Minecraft knows its window size only from
SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_RESIZED, so it kept its requested 854x480 and taps
missed; the window now posts a resize when it got another size than asked.

